      "19279": {
        "comments": [
          {
            "creation_time": "2026-11-03T18:48:32Z",
            "text": "contour() and contourLines() initialize the running maximum of z with zmax = DBL_MIN, the smallest positive double, instead of -DBL_MAX. For a surface whose values are all negative (a log-likelihood, say), zmax stays at about 2e-308, so the perturbation used to move data values off a contour level, atom = 1e-3 * (zmax - zmin), scales with |min(z)| instead of the range of z. Grid points equal to a level are then pushed well away from the drawn contour, and the same surface shifted to positive values is drawn differently. Constant negative z also gives no \"all z values are equal\" warning.\n\nReproducible example: attached as contour-zmax-init-repro.R; the contour at level -999 passes through (2.33, 1), (2, 1.33), ..., whereas the identical surface shifted by +2000 gives (3, 1), (2, 2), (1, 3), expected for both.\n\nAnalysis:\n\nThe line zmax = DBL_MIN has been in the contour code since 1998 (now in both src/main/plot3d.c for contourLines() and src/library/graphics/src/plot3d.c for contour()); zmin on the adjacent line is correctly started at DBL_MAX. Seen on R-devel r90833.\n\nProposed patch: attached as contour-zmax-init.diff. It changes the initialization to -DBL_MAX in both files and adds a regression test; make test-Reg passes.\n\nThe patch is also submitted as https://github.com/r-devel/r-svn/pull/453, where CI results can be seen.",
